\details{
\strong{Pooling.} Counts and pay changes are pooled over all snapshot pairs
before any rate is taken, so a pair with many people at a tenure weighs
more than a pair with few. \code{pay_growth} is
\verb{exp(mean(log(pay next year / pay this year))) - 1} over all stayers at
that tenure.

\strong{Who counts as staying.} Only someone \code{"active"} at the very next
snapshot. Someone missing for a year and back later counted as leaving,
and adds no pay growth across the gap. So does someone who moves to
another status, even with pay in it (e.g. a pension contract).

\strong{Missing or zero pay.} A contract with missing pay is left out of the
person's total, which is missing only if all their contracts are. A
person with missing or zero pay in either year still counts in \code{pop} and
\code{px}, but not in the pay columns.

\strong{Groups that change over time.} A person's groups are those of the
start of each year. Someone who changes occupation adds to one
occupation's rates, then to another's.

\strong{Missing tenure.} An active person-year with no tenure belongs to no
year of the table. It is left out of the rates, with a warning, but still
counts as staying for the person's year before.

\strong{Duplicates.} A person with more than one personnel row in a snapshot
is counted once, with a warning (from \code{\link[=estimate_decrement_rates]{estimate_decrement_rates()}}).

\strong{Not your workforce's future.} The rates describe what happened in the
panel's years. A pay reform or hiring freeze in those years shows up in
them.

Errors if \code{tenure_col} is not in \code{personnel}; if a required column is
missing from either table; if a group column is in both tables or in
neither; or if \code{personnel} has fewer than 2 snapshots.
